# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# (?>X) used as the BODY of an ordinary quantifier: each repetition of the
# quantifier gets its own INDEPENDENT cut (no backtracking within one
# iteration's choice), but the quantifier itself can still backtrack on
# the TOTAL NUMBER of iterations -- which is NOT the same thing as making
# the whole repeated construct possessive (X++ / (?:X)++), where the
# iteration COUNT is locked too. The last pair of blocks below measures
# that distinction directly: same body, same subject, genuinely different
# answers between "(?>X)+" and "(?:X)++". All spans measured against
# libpcre2 10.46 (see README.md).
#
# Every block carries `features atomic-groups`, per atomic_basic.rxt.

# Per-iteration atomic cut still traps exactly like a single (?>...):
# iteration 1 commits to "a" (first alternative), iteration 2 can't start
# (next char is 'b', neither alternative matches), so the + quantifier
# (which already has its required 1 iteration) stops with the cursor
# after "a" -- and there is no "c" there, nor anywhere else in the
# subject once every start position is tried.
pattern (?:(?>a|ab))+c
features atomic-groups
n "abc"

# Here the first alternative's greedy choice per iteration naturally
# lands on the right total count with no need to reduce it afterward.
pattern (?>a|aa)+b
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 0 4

# An atomic, possibly-empty body under * with an empty-iteration guard:
# zero iterations is fine (the group's own first attempt is empty, atomic
# commits to that, * is satisfied with 0).
pattern (?>a?)*b
features atomic-groups
m "b" 0 1

pattern (?>a?)*b
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2

pattern (?>a?)+b
features atomic-groups
m "aab" 0 3

pattern (?:(?>ab|a))+c
features atomic-groups
m "ababc" 0 5

pattern (?:(?>ab|a))+c
features atomic-groups
m "abababc" 0 7

# --- the count-flexibility distinction, measured directly ---
#
# (?>aa|a) repeated by an ordinary +: greedy per-iteration choice takes
# "aa" then "a" (2 iterations, cursor after 3 chars), but the trailing
# "ab" then fails with only "b" left -- so the ORDINARY + backtracks the
# ITERATION COUNT down to 1 (just the initial "aa"), leaving "ab" for the
# literal that follows. This is a normal quantifier-count backtrack; the
# atomicity only ever applied WITHIN each iteration's own alternative
# choice, never across iterations.
pattern (?:(?>aa|a))+ab
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 0 4

# The same body, the same greedy per-iteration process, but the WHOLE
# repeated construct is possessive this time: once ++ commits to its
# iteration count, that count itself cannot be reduced either (not just
# the choice within one iteration) -- so there is no fallback to 1
# iteration, and (checked at every start position in the subject) no
# match anywhere.
pattern (?:aa|a)++ab
features atomic-groups
n "aaab"
